{"name":"Bee’s Knees","hex":"#e8d9d2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bee","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Byangsi."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flying insect, of the clade Anthophila within the hymenopteran superfamily Apoidea, known for its organised societies (though only a minority have them), for collecting pollen and (in some species) producing wax and honey.","Any stinging flying insect, especially a wasp."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bee"},{"term":"knees","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of knee"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of knee"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/knees"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bee's knees","blocks":[{"text":"A bees knees is a Prohibition era cocktail made with gin, fresh lemon juice, and honey. It is served shaken and chilled, often with a lemon twist."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Bee%27s_Knees.jpg/330px-Bee%27s_Knees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee's_knees"},{"title":"Knee","blocks":[{"text":"In humans and other primates, the knee joins the thigh with the leg and consists of two joints: one between the femur and tibia, and one between the femur and patella. It is the largest joint in the human body. The knee is a modified hinge joint, which permits flexion and extension as well as slight internal and external rotation. The knee is vulnerable to injury and to the development of osteoarthritis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Human_knees.jpg/330px-Human_knees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knee"},{"title":"Bee","blocks":[{"text":"Bees are winged insects that form a monophyletic clade Anthophila within the superfamily Apoidea of the order Hymenoptera, with over 20,000 known species in seven recognized families. Some species – including honey bees, bumblebees, and stingless bees – are social insects living in colonies, whereas over 90% of bee species – including mason bees, carpenter bees, leafcutter bees, and sweat bees – are solitary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg/330px-Tetragonula_carbonaria_%2814521993792%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bee"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg/500px-Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg/1280px-Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg/500px-Bees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bees knees cocktail with Barr Hill Gin, honey, and lemon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABees_Knees_Cocktail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Blausen_0597_KneeAnatomy_Side.png/500px-Blausen_0597_KneeAnatomy_Side.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Blausen_0597_KneeAnatomy_Side.png/500px-Blausen_0597_KneeAnatomy_Side.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0597_KneeAnatomy_Side.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Gray351.png/250px-Gray351.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, 